1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,220
This is Doing and Feeling, Episode 5, The Predictive Brain.

2
00:00:04,660 --> 00:00:09,500
Take a hollow mask of a face and turn it so you're looking at its inside, the concave side.

3
00:00:09,740 --> 00:00:12,800
You'd expect to see a hollow. Most people do not.

4
00:00:13,040 --> 00:00:20,320
They see a normal face bulging toward them, and if the mask rotates, the face seems to turn the wrong way, eerily tracking them.

5
00:00:20,720 --> 00:00:22,720
Knowing that it's hollow does not help.

6
00:00:22,960 --> 00:00:27,340
Your brain has an extremely strong expectation that faces are convex,

7
00:00:27,340 --> 00:00:32,280
and the light reaching your eyes is ambiguous enough that the expectation wins.

8
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:38,260
In one small brain imaging study from 2009, with 13 patients and 16 controls,

9
00:00:38,880 --> 00:00:41,640
healthy volunteers were fooled almost every time,

10
00:00:41,880 --> 00:00:45,160
while people with schizophrenia were fooled far less often.

11
00:00:45,420 --> 00:00:51,680
It's a small sample, but it fits the idea that the balance between expectation and evidence is a setting,

12
00:00:51,860 --> 00:00:53,440
and that it can be set differently.

13
00:00:53,900 --> 00:00:57,840
This episode argues that the brain is well described as a prediction machine,

14
00:00:58,040 --> 00:01:02,140
and then shows where that description is solid and where it is still mostly slogan.

15
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:04,900
We'll put the idea through five tests.

16
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:12,340
Perception, the wiring of the cortex, a grand unifying principle, reward, and the brain's internal maps.

17
00:01:12,760 --> 00:01:17,900
It's the bridge between the computational story of the last episode and the machines of the next.

18
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:23,860
The modern form of the idea starts with Hermann von Helmholtz in the 1860s.

19
00:01:24,240 --> 00:01:27,900
Perception, he argued, involves unconscious inference.

20
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:34,440
The brain receives ambiguous signals and infers their most probable causes, drawing on experience.

21
00:01:34,840 --> 00:01:36,880
The modern form is Bayesian.

22
00:01:37,120 --> 00:01:42,080
Combine a likelihood, how well each possible cause would explain the incoming signals,

23
00:01:42,260 --> 00:01:47,060
with a prior, how probable each cause was to begin with, and you get a posterior,

24
00:01:47,900 --> 00:01:53,700
best guess. The hollow mask is what happens when the prior is strong and the likelihood is weak.

25
00:01:53,980 --> 00:01:59,480
Is the brain actually Bayesian? The best evidence comes from experiments on combining the senses.

26
00:01:59,840 --> 00:02:06,000
In 2002, Mark Ernst and Martin Banks had people judge the height of a bar they could both see

27
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:11,840
and feel. When the researchers added noise to the visual display, people leaned more on touch,

28
00:02:11,980 --> 00:02:16,480
and they weighted the two senses almost exactly as an ideal statistician would,

29
00:02:17,900 --> 00:02:23,400
one's reliability. But there is a long list of cases where people are measurably not optimal.

30
00:02:23,700 --> 00:02:30,440
In 2018, Dobromir Ranev and Rachel Denison cataloged them in a Target article for the

31
00:02:30,440 --> 00:02:36,120
journal Behavioral and Brain Sciences and argued that claims of optimality are often flexible

32
00:02:36,120 --> 00:02:41,240
enough to fit almost anything. Their advice was to stop asking whether the brain is optimal

33
00:02:41,240 --> 00:02:46,640
and start building explicit models of what observers actually do. In that journal,

34
00:02:47,900 --> 00:02:50,760
is published together with many short commentaries

35
00:02:50,760 --> 00:02:53,880
from other researchers and a reply from the authors,

36
00:02:53,880 --> 00:02:56,460
which is a good way to see how contested

37
00:02:56,460 --> 00:02:58,100
even this basic claim is.

38
00:02:58,380 --> 00:03:00,800
So is the brain Bayesian or not?

39
00:03:01,420 --> 00:03:03,380
The fairest answer is that it uses

40
00:03:03,380 --> 00:03:06,040
priors and weights evidence by reliability,

41
00:03:06,480 --> 00:03:09,080
approximately, with systematic shortcuts.

42
00:03:09,580 --> 00:03:11,180
Bayesian is a good framework

43
00:03:11,180 --> 00:03:13,100
for asking what the brain is computing.

44
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:16,560
It is not a finding that the brain computes it perfectly.

45
00:03:17,900 --> 00:03:20,140
The second test is the cortex itself.

46
00:03:20,560 --> 00:03:24,140
In 1999, Rajesh Rao and Dana Ballard

47
00:03:24,140 --> 00:03:28,100
proposed an influential model of predictive coding in visual cortex.

48
00:03:28,420 --> 00:03:30,760
The visual system is a hierarchy.

49
00:03:31,220 --> 00:03:34,140
In their model, each level sends predictions down

50
00:03:34,140 --> 00:03:36,020
about what the level below should be seeing

51
00:03:36,020 --> 00:03:38,320
and sends up only the prediction error,

52
00:03:38,560 --> 00:03:40,680
the part of the signal that was not predicted.

53
00:03:40,940 --> 00:03:44,060
When predictions are good, little needs to travel upward.

54
00:03:44,340 --> 00:03:47,540
Their model explained puzzling properties of neurons

55
00:03:47,900 --> 00:03:53,600
visual cortex, such as cells that respond less to a line when it extends beyond the patch they

56
00:03:53,600 --> 00:03:59,140
monitor, because the extension was predictable from context. That's a compression scheme.

57
00:03:59,580 --> 00:04:05,240
Video codecs do the same thing. Send keyframes, then only the differences from what you'd predict.

58
00:04:05,560 --> 00:04:09,760
And the parallel with Episode 3's equivalence of prediction and compression

59
00:04:09,760 --> 00:04:16,500
is no accident. How much of what comes in actually reaches behavior? In a 2024 paper,

60
00:04:17,900 --> 00:04:23,320
Marcus Meister, estimated that human behavior carries about 10 bits of information per second,

61
00:04:23,560 --> 00:04:28,920
while our senses take in something like a billion. They called the gap the unbearable slowness of

62
00:04:28,920 --> 00:04:34,320
being. The authors stress how little anyone understands about that bottleneck, but it is

63
00:04:34,320 --> 00:04:39,680
at least consistent with a brain that keeps a model of its world and passes on mainly what the

64
00:04:39,680 --> 00:04:46,220
model failed to anticipate. The supporting evidence for predictive coding is real. Neural responses

65
00:04:47,900 --> 00:04:59,680
And in 2008, Christopher Summerfield and colleagues showed with functional MRI that this repetition suppression in a face-processing region was stronger when repeats were expected.

66
00:04:59,920 --> 00:05:07,320
A well-known brainwave, the mismatched negativity, appears when a run of identical tones is broken by a deviant one.

67
00:05:07,460 --> 00:05:13,200
And if you omit a tone from a predictable sequence, auditory cortex responds to the silence.

68
00:05:13,480 --> 00:05:16,680
In predictive coding, attention becomes precision.

69
00:05:17,900 --> 00:05:23,360
brain gives to prediction errors from a source, depending on how reliable that source is expected

70
00:05:23,360 --> 00:05:30,300
to be. Now the mess. When Dmitry Kalyakovich and Rufin Vogels recorded single neurons in the

71
00:05:30,300 --> 00:05:35,860
monkey's temporal cortex, repetition suppression did not depend on how likely the repeat was.

72
00:05:36,560 --> 00:05:41,280
Summerfield's own group later called the mismatch between the brain scans and the neurons

73
00:05:41,280 --> 00:05:47,680
unexplained. And there's a live dispute right now. A large preprint led by Jacob Westerberg

74
00:05:47,900 --> 00:05:53,760
and Andre Bastos, recording from Mice and Monkeys, reported that most oddball responses

75
00:05:53,760 --> 00:05:59,960
in sensory cortex are explained by adaptation, neurons simply tiring of repeated input.

76
00:06:00,320 --> 00:06:06,140
Responses to violations of a learned sequence were sparse, came late, and in the monkeys,

77
00:06:06,520 --> 00:06:11,560
appeared first in prefrontal cortex, which suggests the deep prediction errors may be

78
00:06:11,560 --> 00:06:18,100
computed higher up rather than at every level. Then, in January 2026, another preprint from

79
00:06:18,100 --> 00:06:24,720
Jordan Ham's lab found that primary visual cortex in mice does respond to sequence-level surprises,

80
00:06:25,100 --> 00:06:30,420
learned within fewer than 10 repetitions, and that the effect vanished when feedback from a

81
00:06:30,420 --> 00:06:35,900
frontal region, the anterior cingulate, was silenced. Both were posted as preprints,

82
00:06:35,980 --> 00:06:40,640
and they may be measuring somewhat different things. But notice what is being argued about.

83
00:06:41,560 --> 00:06:47,360
in this fight doubts that the brain predicts. In 2025, the International Brain Laboratory

84
00:06:47,360 --> 00:06:52,640
published a brain-wide map of mice making decisions, recorded from more than half a

85
00:06:52,640 --> 00:06:59,700
million neurons across 279 brain areas. It found the animal's prior expectations encoded in at

86
00:06:59,700 --> 00:07:05,340
least a fifth to a third of the regions, at every level from early sensory areas, including the

87
00:07:05,340 --> 00:07:11,460
thalamus, up to motor and frontal cortex. What's in question is the specific textbook circuit,

88
00:07:11,560 --> 00:07:14,840
in which every level computes errors and passes them up.

89
00:07:15,800 --> 00:07:19,100
The third test is the grandest version of the idea.

90
00:07:19,560 --> 00:07:25,400
Starting in the mid-2000s, the neuroscientist Carl Fristen proposed the free energy principle.

91
00:07:25,740 --> 00:07:27,240
Here's the core logic.

92
00:07:27,500 --> 00:07:31,500
Any system that persists, a cell, an organism, a brain,

93
00:07:31,740 --> 00:07:35,320
must stay within a limited set of states compatible with its existence.

94
00:07:35,700 --> 00:07:37,560
A fish must stay in water.

95
00:07:37,560 --> 00:07:42,000
In information theoretic terms, it must avoid surprising states.

96
00:07:42,240 --> 00:07:44,860
But a system cannot measure surprise directly,

97
00:07:44,860 --> 00:07:48,640
because that would require knowing the true probability of everything.

98
00:07:48,980 --> 00:07:53,260
What it can do is minimize a quantity called variational free energy,

99
00:07:53,480 --> 00:07:56,040
which is always at least as large as surprise.

100
00:07:56,580 --> 00:07:59,520
Keep free energy low, and you keep surprise low.

101
00:07:59,820 --> 00:08:03,420
That's the evidence lower bound from variational inference.

102
00:08:03,800 --> 00:08:05,960
Free energy is just its negative.

103
00:08:08,280 --> 00:08:13,540
mathematics. Minimizing it does two things at once. It makes the system's approximate posterior

104
00:08:13,540 --> 00:08:18,100
closer to the true one, and it improves how well the system's model fits its data.

105
00:08:18,640 --> 00:08:24,560
In a variational autoencoder, an encoder network learns to produce that approximate posterior in a

106
00:08:24,560 --> 00:08:30,380
single pass. Predictive coding settles on it gradually by passing errors back and forth.

107
00:08:31,020 --> 00:08:36,780
Friston adds two big ideas. The first is the Markov blanket, a statistical boundary that

108
00:08:37,560 --> 00:08:42,840
system's internal states from the world, mediated by sensory states coming in and active states

109
00:08:42,840 --> 00:08:49,320
going out. The second is active inference. An organism can reduce prediction error in two ways,

110
00:08:49,580 --> 00:08:54,680
change its predictions to fit the world, which is perception, or change the world to fit its

111
00:08:54,680 --> 00:09:00,220
predictions, which is action. When you reach for a cup, on this view, your brain predicts the

112
00:09:00,220 --> 00:09:06,140
sensations of grasping it, and your muscles act to make that prediction come true. To choose actions,

113
00:09:07,560 --> 00:09:12,680
by a related quantity called expected free energy, which combines getting what you prefer

114
00:09:12,680 --> 00:09:18,180
with reducing your uncertainty. That second part is a principled account of curiosity.

115
00:09:18,700 --> 00:09:23,720
There's a classic objection called the dark room problem. If organisms minimize surprise,

116
00:09:24,140 --> 00:09:30,220
why do not we all sit in a dark, quiet room? The standard answer, from a 2012 paper by Friston,

117
00:09:30,640 --> 00:09:35,820
Christopher Thornton, and Andy Clark, is that surprise is relative to an organism's model of

118
00:09:37,560 --> 00:09:42,680
that expects to eat, stay warm and explore, would find a dark room deeply surprising.

119
00:09:43,700 --> 00:09:45,860
The principle has serious critics.

120
00:09:46,220 --> 00:09:52,460
If minimizing free energy covers perception, action, attention and learning all at once,

121
00:09:52,780 --> 00:09:55,260
what observation could ever count against it?

122
00:09:55,520 --> 00:09:59,680
That's the first criticism, and Friston largely agrees, in a sense.

123
00:10:00,120 --> 00:10:05,000
He describes it as a mathematical principle, like the principle of least action in physics,

124
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:09,280
from which testable process theories such as predictive coding can be derived.

125
00:10:09,820 --> 00:10:13,720
Critics reply that a framework that can describe anything explains nothing

126
00:10:13,720 --> 00:10:16,100
until you commit to a specific process theory,

127
00:10:16,220 --> 00:10:18,780
and the process theories are where the evidence lies,

128
00:10:19,040 --> 00:10:21,720
and where, as we just saw, the fights are.

129
00:10:21,960 --> 00:10:23,440
The second is technical.

130
00:10:23,680 --> 00:10:27,940
In a 2022 Behavioral and Brain Sciences target article

131
00:10:27,940 --> 00:10:30,360
titled The Emperor's New Markov Blankets,

132
00:10:30,840 --> 00:10:33,180
Jell Bruinberg and colleagues argued

133
00:10:33,180 --> 00:10:36,400
that the field uses Markov blankets in two senses.

134
00:10:36,720 --> 00:10:40,680
One is a formal tool from statistics, a property of a model.

135
00:10:40,900 --> 00:10:43,880
The other is a claim about real physical boundaries

136
00:10:43,880 --> 00:10:46,520
between organisms and their environments.

137
00:10:47,100 --> 00:10:49,060
Sliding between them, they argue,

138
00:10:49,380 --> 00:10:51,980
makes the framework seem to deliver more than it does.

139
00:10:52,220 --> 00:10:55,100
The third is a tension with reinforcement learning,

140
00:10:55,360 --> 00:10:57,620
which says agents maximize reward.

141
00:10:58,420 --> 00:11:02,240
Friston's reply is that rewards can be recast as prior preferences.

142
00:11:02,640 --> 00:11:07,060
The organism expects to be fed, so being fed is unsurprising.

143
00:11:07,460 --> 00:11:12,700
Whether that recasting adds insight or just relabels the problem is still argued about.

144
00:11:13,560 --> 00:11:17,280
The fourth test is reward, and here the brain offers

145
00:11:17,280 --> 00:11:21,520
what may be the clearest machine learning algorithm ever found in biology.

146
00:11:21,880 --> 00:11:27,020
In the 1990s, Wolfram Schultz recorded dopamine neurons in monkeys' learning

147
00:11:27,620 --> 00:11:32,700
predicted a squirt of juice. At first, the neurons fired a burst when the juice arrived.

148
00:11:33,140 --> 00:11:38,260
After learning, the burst moved to the queue, and the juice itself produced no response.

149
00:11:38,580 --> 00:11:44,140
And if the expected juice failed to arrive, the neurons dipped below baseline at exactly the

150
00:11:44,140 --> 00:11:50,040
moment it should have come. In 1997, Schultz, Peter Dayen, and Reed Montague pointed out that

151
00:11:50,040 --> 00:11:55,340
this is precisely the reward prediction error of temporal difference learning, an algorithm Richard

152
00:11:55,340 --> 00:12:00,820
Sutton had developed for machines. The reward you got, plus the value of where you ended up,

153
00:12:01,140 --> 00:12:07,420
minus the value you predicted. Once the Q reliably predicts juice, the juice is no longer news.

154
00:12:07,700 --> 00:12:13,920
The news arrives with the Q, and so does the burst. The same algorithm had powered TDgammon,

155
00:12:14,120 --> 00:12:19,840
a backgammon program from the early 90s. The correspondence has deepened. In 2020,

156
00:12:19,840 --> 00:12:22,820
while Dabney and colleagues at DeepMind and Harvard

157
00:12:22,820 --> 00:12:27,760
found that dopamine neurons encode not one expected reward, but a distribution.

158
00:12:28,400 --> 00:12:32,320
Some neurons are tuned to be optimistic, others pessimistic,

159
00:12:32,500 --> 00:12:36,740
and the population as a whole represents the range of possible rewards,

160
00:12:36,980 --> 00:12:40,160
exactly as in distributional reinforcement learning,

161
00:12:40,420 --> 00:12:43,060
which had been invented a few years earlier for AI.

162
00:12:43,460 --> 00:12:49,680
An algorithm first tested on Atari games turned out to fit dopamine recordings in mice.

163
00:12:49,840 --> 00:12:56,480
That could mean the brain runs it, or just that evolution and engineers found the same good solution.

164
00:12:56,860 --> 00:12:58,940
Either way, the match keeps growing.

165
00:12:59,300 --> 00:13:07,700
In 2025, a study in Nature reported that dopamine neurons jointly map when a reward will arrive and how big it will be.

166
00:13:07,840 --> 00:13:10,340
But even here the slogan has a challenger.

167
00:13:10,340 --> 00:13:17,380
In 2022, H. Wee-Yung-Yung, Vijay Nambudiri, and colleagues argued, from experiments in mice,

168
00:13:17,660 --> 00:13:22,020
that dopamine release looks more like a signal of learned causal associations,

169
00:13:22,420 --> 00:13:26,560
worked out by looking backward from rewards, than a forward prediction error.

170
00:13:26,880 --> 00:13:31,420
The prediction error account remains the best-supported algorithm in biology,

171
00:13:31,840 --> 00:13:33,960
and it is still contested at the edges.

172
00:13:34,940 --> 00:13:36,820
The fifth test is MAPS.

173
00:13:36,820 --> 00:13:44,000
In 1948, Edward Tallman argued from experiments with rats in mazes that animals build cognitive maps,

174
00:13:44,260 --> 00:13:47,480
internal models that let them take shortcuts they've never tried.

175
00:13:47,980 --> 00:13:56,060
The neural evidence arrived in 1971, when John O'Keefe and Jonathan Dostrovsky found placed cells in the rat hippocampus,

176
00:13:56,460 --> 00:13:59,960
neurons that fire when the animal is in a particular location.

177
00:14:00,180 --> 00:14:05,960
In 2005, Maybritt and Edvard Moser's lab found grid cells in the nearby entorhinal cortex,

178
00:14:06,820 --> 00:14:12,400
fire at many locations arranged in a strikingly regular triangular lattice, like coordinate lines

179
00:14:12,400 --> 00:14:19,240
laid over the world. O'Keefe and the Mosers shared the 2014 Nobel Prize. Nearby are head direction

180
00:14:19,240 --> 00:14:25,240
cells, which work like a compass, and boundary cells, which fire near walls. Together they form

181
00:14:25,240 --> 00:14:31,520
something close to a navigation system. These maps predict. During rest and sleep, the hippocampus

182
00:14:31,520 --> 00:14:37,780
replay sequences of place cell activity, compressed in time, including paths the animal took and

183
00:14:37,780 --> 00:14:44,380
sometimes paths it has not. In 2017, Kimberly Stachenfeld, Matthew Botvinik, and Samuel

184
00:14:44,380 --> 00:14:49,720
Jerschman proposed that the hippocampus encodes each place by where you are likely to go next

185
00:14:49,720 --> 00:14:55,760
from it, what reinforcement learning calls a successor representation. That explains why

186
00:14:55,760 --> 00:15:00,740
place fields stretch backward along a habitual route. And the maps are not only spatial.

187
00:15:01,520 --> 00:15:16,840
In 2016, Alexandra Konstantiniscou, Jill O'Reilly, and Timothy Behrens taught people an abstract space of cartoon birds whose necks and legs varied in length and found grid-like brain signals as people mentally moved through that conceptual space.

188
00:15:17,080 --> 00:15:22,400
The brain seems to reuse its navigation machinery to organize knowledge in general.

189
00:15:22,920 --> 00:15:28,900
James Whittington, Behrens, and colleagues then built a model called the Talman-Eichenbaum machine

190
00:15:28,900 --> 00:15:33,320
that learns to separate the structure of an environment from its sensory contents.

191
00:15:33,600 --> 00:15:37,160
So the same structural knowledge carries over to new places,

192
00:15:37,500 --> 00:15:40,920
and its units developed place-like and grid-like responses.

193
00:15:41,260 --> 00:15:46,740
In 2022, Whittington, Joseph Warren, and Behrens showed that transformers

194
00:15:46,740 --> 00:15:52,440
with a particular kind of position encoding are closely related, mathematically, to that model,

195
00:15:52,600 --> 00:15:58,900
and reproduce the same cell types. A model of the hippocampus and the architecture of modern AI

196
00:15:58,900 --> 00:16:06,540
turned out to be cousins. So what survives the five tests? Perception combines priors with evidence,

197
00:16:06,900 --> 00:16:12,060
roughly in proportion to reliability. The cortex is full of expectation signals,

198
00:16:12,060 --> 00:16:14,680
even if the textbook circuit is under dispute.

199
00:16:14,900 --> 00:16:17,640
The free energy principle is better described

200
00:16:17,640 --> 00:16:21,360
as a powerful mathematical lens than as a testable theory.

201
00:16:21,640 --> 00:16:24,900
Reward learning is the clearest case of a machine algorithm

202
00:16:24,900 --> 00:16:26,320
running in biology.

203
00:16:26,640 --> 00:16:29,260
And the hippocampus builds predictive maps,

204
00:16:29,400 --> 00:16:31,980
and one leading model of them turns out to be

205
00:16:31,980 --> 00:16:34,520
a close mathematical cousin of the transformer.

206
00:16:34,960 --> 00:16:38,620
In every case, the brain is learning a model of its world

207
00:16:38,620 --> 00:16:41,380
and using it to anticipate what comes next.

208
00:16:42,060 --> 00:16:44,520
and all of it could happen without anyone home.

209
00:16:44,920 --> 00:16:47,820
Nothing in this episode needed the word experience.

210
00:16:48,320 --> 00:16:50,100
That's the point to hold on to.

211
00:16:50,380 --> 00:16:56,380
Some theories we'll meet in episode 11 argue that experience is made of exactly this kind of prediction,

212
00:16:56,820 --> 00:16:59,460
especially prediction about keeping a body alive.

213
00:16:59,920 --> 00:17:02,300
Others say prediction is beside the point.

214
00:17:02,480 --> 00:17:07,760
But first, the machines, how they learn, and what researchers have found inside them.

